Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President.
The United States welcomes the extension of the UN DRC sanctions regime for another year. Our renewal today of the Group of Experts’ (GOE) mandate, as well as the arms embargo, travel ban, and assets freeze measures, are an important step to advancing our collective goal of peace in eastern DRC.
We particularly welcome the resolution’s reference to natural resource exploitation. Illicit mineral extraction fuels conflict in the DRC. The Group of Experts’ reporting is vital to tracing these illicit activities and identifying those actively involved. If the Committee is serious about addressing contributing factors to conflict, it is critical that we pursue those trafficking conflict minerals.
We also appreciate the Secretary-General’s continued commitment to support justice efforts on behalf of Michael Sharp and Zaida Catalan. We welcome recent developments in the DRC to hold key individuals to account. We echo the call in the resolution to continue efforts to bring everyone—including those involved in the planning, directing, or sponsoring of the killings—to justice. We continue to monitor progress closely.
Finally, we welcome the inclusion of a new listing criterion that allows the Council to designate entities that obstruct the implementation of MONUSCO’s mandate—a meaningful step toward strengthening accountability.
The United States remains committed to a robust and credible DRC sanctions regime—one that reflects ground realities, protects civilians, and promotes accountability for those who perpetuate violence and instability in the region.
Thank you.
