Remarks at an Online Symposium in the UN on the Abductions Issue on the theme “Opening a Window of Opportunity for the Immediate Resolution of the Abductions Issue”
Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Hello, I want to thank Ambassador Yamazaki and the Government of Japan for leading today’s symposium, which keeps attention where it belongs on the victims of abductions and their families.
The U.S. is proud to co-host alongside Japan, and the Republic of Korea, Australia, and the European Union.
This issue demands the world’s attention because it is so clear there is no question. Parents lost children, husbands lost wives, families lost decades with their loved ones, and North Korea has still never told the truth.
To the family members here today: Thank you. Your courage is simply remarkable. Year after year, you have carried photographs, told your stories, and refused, absolutely refused, to let the world look away. You should never have had to become advocates. You should have your loved ones at home with you right now, but even now you continue to tell the truth. And the United States stands with you, stands with Japan, with the Republic of Korea, and with every nation whose citizens were stolen by the DPRK.
This was state-sponsored kidnapping. Full stop. The DPRK stole innocent people from their families, dragged them into darkness, spent decades lying about it, and still, even today, won’t tell the truth.
Yokota Megumi was just 13 years old when she was taken. 13. A child just walking home from school and then disappeared and never came home.
Taguchi Yaeko, a young mother, was taken from her family. Her children were robbed of their mother, and she was robbed of the life she should have lived with them.
Both of them deserve to return home now.
President Trump has also been clear, and he has personally invested in the release of DPRK abductees. When he met with abductee family members last October, he promised that the United States will do everything in its power to help reunite them with their loved ones. President Trump keeps his promises, and I echo that commitment today.
We know this regime’s playbook. North Korea has also detained our citizens, they’ve abused them, and they’ve used their lives as leverage in this sick approach to international relations.
We call again for the immediate resolution of all abduction cases. The DPRK must provide a full accounting, return those still held, and give the families the truth now. This disgusting chapter of history must end, and it must end forever. The world should say plainly, and the world should join all of us in saying that no government, gets to kidnap innocent people, lie about it for decades, and then expect normal treatment by the world community.
To the families, you are not alone. The American people stand with you. Your courage continues to inspire us, our diplomats, and our president, all of whom are doing our utmost to return your loved ones. And we will keep pressing until every abductee is accounted for, and every family gets the truth that they have been denied for far too long. Your advocacy gives hope that together we will bring them home. I commend you, we stand with you, and I thank you.
