U.S. Statement at the Economic and Social Council, Humanitarian Affairs Segment, Side Event “Delivering on International Humanitarian Law: Mobilizing Action to Protect Health Care in Conflict”
Meghan Hanson
Deputy Assistant Secretary of State
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you Chair and panelists for your briefings.
Ten years since resolution 2286, the United States reiterates the clear demand for operationalization of efforts to prevent attacks in armed conflict on medical personnel and protected facilities. We are appalled by the robust data that shows an increase in attacks on healthcare in conflicts since 2016.
We call on all Member States to take accountability seriously and to move the 2286 agenda forward. And we reaffirm that accountability is a critical requirement when attacks on medical care in armed conflict are committed.
It is important to underscore that while accountability includes the prosecution of perpetrators, it does not stop there. Accountability exists on a spectrum. Accountability can be institutional, such as adaptations of military doctrine, revised training, and active efforts to learn lessons from mistakes. Simply acknowledging an incident itself is a form of accountability and can be meaningful to impacted communities.
The spectrum of accountability also means sharing what we learn. The United States military undertakes significant efforts to mitigate civilian harm in armed conflict, including inadvertent harm to medical personnel and protected facilities. We work closely with our Allies and Partners to share these lessons widely and strengthen our collective efforts to better protect civilians and the humanitarians who are providing life-saving services to civilians in armed conflict.
Tracking and reporting are also essential for accountability and lessons learned collection. A more robust reporting mechanism for misuse of protected facilities in conflict is essential and can support information integrity in contested environments. Current reporting models are insufficient to capture when terrorists and armed groups violate the protected status of hospitals. Attacking hospitals is a violation of international humanitarian law and systematically erodes both the protection of such facilities and the trust of the public in the level of protection international humanitarian law can provide.
Efforts to improve the protection of medical personnel deserves our sustained attention. The United States stands ready to partner with delegations similarly committed to this effort.
Thank you.
