Ambassador Dan Negrea
Representative of the United States on the Economic and Social Council of the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Administrator.
As we look ahead, the global landscape demands that development models shift toward supporting self-reliance, long-term resilience, and economic growth.
To achieve this, the United States emphasizes a critical shift in development philosophy: prioritizing Trade over Aid. We must consciously move away from traditional government-to-government aid models. Instead, we call for mutually beneficial business partnerships between the private sectors of partner countries.
Let me make this very clear, just like America First does not mean America alone, Trade over Aid does not mean no aid. America remains the world’s most generous nation. Since December 2025, we contributed $3.9 billion to OCHA in humanitarian aid. We are also continuing development aid through hundreds of millions of dollars of strategic economic investments in countries around the world.
But the emphasis should be on trade. True progress is achieved by enabling sovereign nations to own their development trajectories and chart their own pro-growth courses by expanding free market principles, fostering pro-business governance models, and cultivating local innovation rather than creating permanent dependency.
UNDP plays a valuable role when its interventions align with these objectives—specifically, by helping program countries build the robust domestic regulatory and other architecture required to attract commercial investment.
We support UNDP’s capacity-building efforts that lower the barrier to entry for the private sector. True pro-growth reforms require a laser focus on strengthening business-enabling environments through efforts such as:
o Strengthening property rights
o Facilitating free capital flows
o Building credible, fair judicial and legal systems
o A tax code that incentives businesses and entrepreneurs in enabling them to earn a profit and reinvest it in businesses and the wider economy.
When UNDP works to support business enabling environments, it unlocks the transformative scale of market-led development. UNDP must avoid though mandate creep and initiatives that support gender ideology and soft global governance requirements like those in the SDG 2030 Agenda.
We note the difficult financial and staffing decisions that UNDP has already made to meet with current fiscal realities. To demonstrate alignment with these fiscal realities and ensure that maximum resources are devoted to development impacts on the ground, we must lead by example. We urge UNDP to institutionalize stricter cost-saving measures, such as an outright ban on business class travel. Fiscal prudence internally underscores credibility externally.
The United States looks forward to working collaboratively with UNDP to pivot toward a future defined by trade, investment, partnerships, and enduring economic independence.
