Jake Sherman
Minister Counselor for UN Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you Executive Director Da Silva. Also, I wanted to take a moment in particular to thank all of UNOPS for the work that they do in extremely difficult contexts. The United States welcomes this opportunity to engage with UNOPS on its operational performance and strategic direction.
We appreciate UNOPS for its forward-leaning work and operational agility in response to the world’s most complex crises. In an era of compounding global volatility, our partnership has yielded vital lifelines. In particular, the United States emphasizes that the UNOPS-managed 2720 mechanism has served a crucial role in monitoring the delivery of humanitarian assistance to Gaza throughout the logistical supply chain in a transparent and accountable way.
We recognize UNOPS’s efforts in providing viable options and technical frameworks for a humanitarian corridor through the Strait of Hormuz to protect global food and energy security. Iran cannot be allowed to extort the global community and hold the world economy hostage.
Executing these critical missions effectively requires an absolute commitment to internal optimization. To that end, the United States strongly supports UNOPS’s work to become fit for purpose, first through the Comprehensive Response Plan and now through efforts to advance comprehensive institutional reforms aligned with the UN80 Initiative.
Faced with severe global budget constraints, the UN system must move away from fragmentation and toward a simpler, more coherent architecture. We expect UNOPS, and all UN agencies, to fully embrace the reform agenda outlined in UN80, optimizing value-for-money and ensuring that precious taxpayer dollars flow directly to the frontlines of crisis response rather than bureaucracy.
Finally, navigating these fiscal realities demands that the UN system leads by example. Internal culture must match external frugality. To maximize the impact of every dollar entrusted to the organization, we urge UNOPS to implement rigorous cost-saving measures immediately, including an explicit, outright ban on business-class travel for all personnel and leadership.
