Office of Press and Public Diplomacy
For Immediate Release
New York – U.S. Representative to the United Nations Mike Waltz and U.S. Representative to the United Nations Economic and Social Council Dan Negrea laid out the Trump Administration’s new America First development agenda in an op-ed titled, “Trade Over Aid: Prosperity for America and the World.”
For decades, Washington poured taxpayer dollars into foreign aid programs that produced dependency, bureaucracy, and excuses. President Trump is changing that.
The State Department’s new “Trade Over Aid” initiative puts American strength, private enterprise, and hard-headed results back at the center of development policy. The mission is simple: help nations stand on their own feet through trade, investment, and commercial partnership instead of endless government-run aid programs that keep countries dependent and American taxpayers on the hook.
The initiative launched at the New York Stock Exchange and was heralded by over 35 countries from every corner of the globe—each of which signed on last month to support the new model built on prosperity, self-reliance, and doing business with the United States.
“Mutually beneficial trade, investment, and commercial cooperation among partners are a better route to progress,” Ambassadors Waltz and Negrea write. “We are calling on all nations to get into the business of doing business together—and especially with the United States. Modernizing the world’s approach to development aid is long overdue. Through Trade Over Aid, we can create real results, advance prosperity for all nations, and achieve a freer world.”
President Trump is putting America first, demanding results from the UN, and using American economic power to create opportunity instead of dependency.
